 ],
 "literal": "Black-eyed male monkey great departure having attained, / widowhood not escaping loving female monkey, / unlearned strong young cub at the fork of a branch having placed, / rising-high mountain cleft having leaped, life destroys, / O lord of the slope land! At midnight / do not come, may you live! We suffer, we indeed.",
 "translation": "When her black-eyed male monkey dies, the loving female monkey, unable to survive her widowhood, leaves her young, untaught cub at the fork of a branch, and leaps from the high mountain cleft to end her life. O lord of such mountain slopes! Do not come at midnight. May you live long! We only suffer in anxiety for you.",
 "modern": "கரிய கண்களை உடைய ஆண் குரங்கு இறந்துவிட்டதால், கைம்மைத் துயரத்தைத் தாங்க முடியாத அன்பான பெண் குரங்கு, தன் அறிவில்லாத குட்டியை மரக்கிளையின் கவட்டிலே சேர்த்துவிட்டு, உயர்ந்த மலையின் அடுக்கிலிருந்து குதித்துத் தன் உயிரை மாய்த்துக் கொள்ளும். அத்தகைய மலைச்சாரல் நாட்டுத் தலைவனே! நடு இரவில் நீ எங்களை நாடி வர வேண்டாம். நீ டூழி வாழ்க! உனது ஆபத்தான பயணத்தை நினைத்து நாங்கள் வருந்துகிறோம்.",
 "explanation": "The companion (tōḻi) speaks to the hero (talaivaṉ) who has been coming secretly at midnight to meet the heroine (talaivi). She describes the mountain slopes of his land, where a female monkey, unable to endure the death of her mate, leaves her young cub safely on a branch and leaps to her death. This poignant image of the monkey's devotion and tragic end serves as an implied metaphor (ullurai), warning the hero of the dangers of his nightly journeys. The companion implies that if anything happens to him on his perilous path, the heroine will not survive either, just like the devoted female monkey. By wishing him well ('vāḻiyō') while asking him not to come at night ('vāral'), she urges him to seek a formal public marriage instead of secret trysts.",
